---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatXml from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatXml.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="xml-escape">
  <DevFormatXml client:load mode="escape" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The five characters XML escape handles</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Character</th><th>Entity</th><th>Required in text?</th><th>Required in an attribute?</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>&amp;</code></td><td><code>&amp;amp;</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&lt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;lt;</code></td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&gt;</code></td><td><code>&amp;gt;</code></td><td>Only after <code>]]</code></td><td>No</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>"</code></td><td><code>&amp;quot;</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes, if the attribute uses double quotes</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>'</code></td><td><code>&amp;apos;</code></td><td>No</td><td>Yes, if the attribute uses single quotes</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      The tool escapes the greater-than sign by default, although it is seldom required, because of the
      <code>]]&gt;</code> sequence: in text, a parser reads it as the end of a CDATA section that was never
      opened. Escaping every <code>&gt;</code> prevents this for three extra characters, and most serialisers
      do the same.
    </p>

    <h2>Escape at insertion, never afterwards</h2>
    <p>
      Escape a value when you put it into markup, and escape only that value. If you escape a whole document
      after you assemble it, the markup you wrote is escaped twice. If you escape before you store data, the
      escaped form goes to every other consumer of that data, such as a report, a CSV export or a search index,
      where it appears as literal <code>&amp;amp;</code> text.
    </p>
    <p>
      In practice, use your XML library&rsquo;s text-node API, not string concatenation. Use this page when you
      write a fragment by hand, paste a value into a config file, or debug why a value came out wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Do not escape non-ASCII</h2>
    <p>
      Some older tools convert every accented letter to a numeric reference, such as <code>&amp;#233;</code>
      for é. XML documents are Unicode, normally UTF-8, so this is not necessary: it makes the file larger and
      harder to read and diff. Declare the encoding in the XML declaration and type the characters.
    </p>

    <h2>CDATA is not a substitute</h2>
    <p>
      A CDATA section lets you include raw <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&amp;</code> without escaping, so people
      use it to embed HTML or a script in XML. It has two problems. The sequence <code>]]&gt;</code> cannot
      appear inside CDATA, and there is no escape for it, so you must split the section. And CDATA is only a
      lexical shortcut, not a data type: to a parser the content is ordinary text, so XPath or a DOM API reads
      the same string either way. Escaping is simpler and always works.
    </p>

    <h2>XML escaping versus HTML escaping</h2>
    <p>
      They overlap but you cannot swap them. Text escaped for XML is always safe in HTML, because the five XML
      entities all exist in HTML. The reverse is not true: HTML has about two thousand named entities, and an
      XML parser rejects each one it does not know. To move content from HTML into XML, run it through
      <a href="/xml-unescape">unescape</a> first and escape it again here.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
